{
  // `lib` is ES2024 and nothing else, and `types` is empty — copied from
  // packages/domain, for a reason this package states one notch more strongly.
  //
  // docs/architecture.md requires packages/physics to depend on no rendering,
  // BLE or platform API: it is pure computation, and the same model has to
  // produce the same metre on a phone, in a browser and on a Phase 3 instance.
  // Without the DOM lib `document`, `window` and `requestAnimationFrame` are
  // compile errors here; with `types: []`, so are `process` and `Buffer`, even
  // though @types/node is installed at the workspace root and would otherwise
  // be found by the default `@types` lookup.
  //
  // ⚠️ **`Date` and `Math.random` survive this narrowing**, because both are
  // ECMAScript built-ins and are in `lib: ["ES2024"]` — exactly as `DataView`
  // survives it in packages/sensors. A physics tick that reads a clock is not
  // deterministic and is not time-step independent, which is a whole acceptance
  // criterion of #88, so the ban on those two is enforced in eslint.config.js
  // (`no-restricted-globals` for `Date`, `no-restricted-properties` for
  // `Math.random`) and NOT here. Do not read this file as covering them.
  //
  // The closure is conditional on nothing in this program pulling a
  // `/// <reference types="node" />` back in through a `.d.ts` — which is why
  // `vitest.config.ts`, which is in the program, imports nothing. Its own
  // header records why at length (CLAUDE.md §4d).
  "extends": "../../tsconfig.base.json",
  "compilerOptions": {
    "lib": ["ES2024"],
    "types": []
  },
  "include": ["src/**/*.ts", "vitest.config.ts"]
}
